      <p class="text-theme-primary text-lg leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Quick answer:</strong> Agricultural wire mesh is selected by animal, crop, and exposure. For perimeter farm fencing, use <strong>hinge-joint field fence</strong> (heights 0.8–2.4 m, top/bottom wires 2.5–3.0 mm, hot-dip galvanized ≥40 g/m² zinc). For cattle and horse enclosures, welded <strong>livestock panels</strong> (1.8 m × 2.1–4.2 m, 5–7 rails) outperform plain mesh in impact resistance. For chicken and rabbit cages, <strong>hexagonal mesh</strong> with 13–25 mm openings in BWG 20–22 wire is the standard. For crop protection, specify HDPE or PP knotted netting (bird net 18–25 mm mesh, insect net 0.4–0.6 mm aperture) rather than metal mesh. In soil contact or high-moisture barns, upgrade to 200+ g/m² zinc coating or Galfan wire—a $0.05/m² upgrade that triples fence life.
      </p>
