    <!-- Farm Fencing -->
    <section id="farm-fencing" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Farm Fencing: Field Fence Knots and Hinge Joints</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Field fence (also called farm fence, cattle fence, or hinge-joint fence) is the default perimeter solution for cattle, sheep, goat, and deer operations. It is a roll of horizontal wires joined by vertical stay wires, with graduated spacing—tight 10–15 cm openings at the bottom (to stop lambs, kids, and piglets) widening to 20–30 cm at the top. The knot that joins horizontal and vertical wires is what separates a 20-year fence from a 5-year fence.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Three knot types dominate the market:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Hinge joint (H.J.):</strong> The horizontal wire wraps around the stay wire. Under animal impact the joint flexes and springs back. Cheapest knot; adequate for sheep and light cattle. The flexing point is also the wear point—expect 10–15 year life with standard galvanizing.</li>
        <li><strong>Fixed knot (S knot / square deal knot):</strong> A separate short wire is wrapped around both the horizontal and stay wires, locking them rigidly. No flex point, so coating abrasion is minimal and the fence holds tension far better under bull or deer pressure. Fixed-knot fence costs 15–25% more than hinge joint and lasts roughly twice as long in the same environment.</li>
        <li><strong>Chain link (diamond mesh):</strong> Not a field fence, but common for smaller paddocks, dog runs, and around buildings. See our <a href="/products/chain-link-fence/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">chain link fence product page</a> for wire gauges and selvage options.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Standard field fence specifications from Hebei factories:
      </p>

