    <!-- Livestock Panels -->
    <section id="livestock-panels" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Livestock Panels and Horse Fencing</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Where field fence is tensioned between posts, <strong>livestock panels</strong> (corral panels, cattle hurdles) are rigid pre-welded frames that drop into pin-connected posts or brackets. They handle direct animal impact—kicking, rubbing, leaning—without stretching, which is why every feedlot, auction yard, and rodeo arena uses them. Because welds and tubes are fully hot-dip galvanized after fabrication, panel life in outdoor service is 15–25 years.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Standard export panels from Anping:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Cattle panels:</strong> 1.8 m high × 2.1–4.2 m long; 5–7 horizontal rails of 30×60 mm oval tube or 32–42 mm round tube; vertical posts 50×50 mm square; rails welded flush inside the frame.</li>
        <li><strong>Horse panels:</strong> Same frame, but rail spacing capped at 20–30 cm with no V-shaped gaps a hoof can catch in. Oval rails are preferred over round for horses because they present no top edge to rear up on.</li>
        <li><strong>Hog/sheep panels:</strong> 0.9–1.1 m high, 16–20 cm rail spacing at the bottom, sometimes with vertical mesh infill below the first rail.</li>
        <li><strong>Finishing gates and bow gates:</strong> Match panel height; spec the latch type (single-lever slam latch is the feedlot standard).</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Two quality checks matter on panels. First, <strong>zinc coverage at welds</strong>: cheap panels are welded from pre-galvanized tube, leaving every weld unprotected rust points that fail in 2–3 years. Insist on hot-dip galvanizing <em>after</em> welding (specify "HDG after fabrication, zinc ≥450 g/m² total both sides"). Second, <strong>tube wall thickness</strong>: 1.6 mm wall is the honest minimum for cattle; 1.2 mm panels dent under a leaning bull. A wall-thickness gauge check at receiving inspection catches this in seconds.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Cage Wire -->
    <section id="cage-wire" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Chicken and Rabbit Cage Wire: Hexagonal Mesh</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        For poultry runs, rabbit hutches, and small-animal enclosures, <strong>hexagonal wire mesh</strong> (chicken wire, hex netting) is the economical choice. Wires twist around each other at 60 degrees forming hexagons, which lets the mesh conform to uneven ground and absorb pecking and gnawing without breaking welds. It is available galvanized, PVC-coated (green or black), or in stainless for premium breeding operations.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Sizing hex netting for cages:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Animal</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Mesh Opening</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Wire Diameter</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Height</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Chicks / quail</td>
              <td class="p-3">13 mm (1/2 in)</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.5–0.7 mm (BWG 24–22)</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.5–1.0 m</td>
              <td class="p-3">PVC coated resists rust in damp brooders</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Laying hens</td>
              <td class="p-3">25 mm (1 in)</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.7–0.9 mm (BWG 22–20)</td>
              <td class="p-3">1.0–1.8 m</td>
              <td class="p-3">Add 30 cm apron buried against predators</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Rabbits</td>
              <td class="p-3">13–19 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.7–1.0 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.6–1.0 m</td>
              <td class="p-3">Bottom of hutch: 13 mm keeps kits in</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Geese / turkeys</td>
              <td class="p-3">40–50 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.9–1.2 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">1.5–1.8 m</td>
              <td class="p-3">Stronger wire resists pushing and flapping</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Gauge confusion is the classic buying error here. Hex netting is sold by <strong>BWG (Birmingham Wire Gauge)</strong>, where a higher number means a thinner wire—and the difference between BWG 20 (0.89 mm) and BWG 22 (0.71 mm) is a 37% difference in steel area and strength. Always convert to millimeters on the purchase order. Use our <a href="/tools/wire-gauge-conversion/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">wire gauge conversion tool</a> to translate BWG/SWG/AWG to millimeters before writing your RFQ.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Zinc-coated hex netting is produced to <strong>ASTM A390</strong> (poultry fence fabric) in export markets, with coating class dictating service life. Class 1 coating (min ~105 g/m²) suits indoor cages and short outdoor runs; Class 3 (min ~245 g/m²) is the right choice for permanent outdoor runs. Full product options are on our <a href="/products/hexagonal-wire-mesh/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">hexagonal wire mesh product page</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Crop Protection -->
    <section id="crop-protection" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Crop Protection and Trellis Mesh</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Crop protection is one place where metal mesh is usually the <em>wrong</em> material. Knotted HDPE or PP netting gives you the same barrier at one-fifth the weight, will not corrode, and packs into 20 kg bales that load efficiently. Metal still wins for trellis and support structures, where the load is mechanical, not just a barrier.
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Bird netting:</strong> Knotted HDPE, 18×18 mm or 25×25 mm mesh, 15–30 g/m², UV-stabilized (spec 3–5% carbon black or HALS UV package, 5+ season warranty). Installed over orchards, berry tunnels, and vineyards. Anti-bird net must be knotted, not extruded—extruded diamond net unravels at one cut.</li>
        <li><strong>Hail netting:</strong> Woven HDPE 7–8 g/m² with 4–7 mm aperture; 2–3 season life but saves the crop when it counts.</li>
        <li><strong>Insect netting:</strong> Woven mesh with 0.4–0.6 mm aperture (roughly 40–50 mesh) excludes aphids, whitefly, and thrips from tunnels; airflow loss is the tradeoff, so size tunnels wider.</li>
        <li><strong>Windbreak shade net:</strong> 40–50% shade rating for nurseries and livestock shade structures; knitted construction resists tearing at grommets.</li>
        <li><strong>Trellis wire:</strong> For vineyards, hop yards, and espalier fruit, use high-tensile galvanized steel wire, 2.2–3.5 mm diameter, 1,100–1,570 MPa tensile, ≥200 g/m² zinc. Do not substitute soft field-fence wire—trellis wire carries crop load for 20+ years and must hold tension season after season. Welded wire panels ( grid welded mesh ) also serve as rigid trellis and tree guards.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Common Mistake</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          Buying bird net by length without checking the <strong>weight per m²</strong>. Two nets with identical mesh size can differ 2× in weight; the lighter one is a one-season product. Specify gsm (grams per square meter) and UV warranty years, not just dimensions.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Coatings -->
    <section id="coatings" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Coating Choices for Soil and Animal Exposure</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Coating selection drives agricultural mesh lifespan more than any other spec. The environment classes that matter: above-ground dry (barn interiors, grain stores), above-ground humid (outdoor pasture, rainfall), soil contact (buried aprons, ground staples, vineyard posts), and animal contact (saliva, manure, rubbing). Match coating to the worst environment the mesh will see.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Coating</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Zinc Weight</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Outdoor Life*</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Soil Contact</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Typical Use</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Electro-galvanized</td>
              <td class="p-3">8–15 g/m²</td>
              <td class="p-3">1–3 years</td>
              <td class="p-3">No</td>
              <td class="p-3">Indoor cages only</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Hot-dip galvanized (standard)</td>
              <td class="p-3">40–60 g/m²</td>
              <td class="p-3">5–10 years</td>
              <td class="p-3">Marginal</td>
              <td class="p-3">Field fence, panels in dry climates</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Hot-dip galvanized (heavy)</td>
              <td class="p-3">200–275 g/m²</td>
              <td class="p-3">20–30 years</td>
              <td class="p-3">Yes</td>
              <td class="p-3">Soil-contact fence, trellis, staples</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Galfan (Zn-5%Al)</td>
              <td class="p-3">95–250 g/m²</td>
              <td class="p-3">2–3× vs HDG at same weight</td>
              <td class="p-3">Yes, best in class</td>
              <td class="p-3">Coastal farms, barns, humid regions</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">PVC coated (on galvanized core)</td>
              <td class="p-3">Core ≥40 g/m² + 0.4–0.6 mm polymer</td>
              <td class="p-3">10–15 years</td>
              <td class="p-3">Yes, if coating unbroken</td>
              <td class="p-3">Chicken runs, garden fence, visible fence lines</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm mb-6">
        *Life ranges assume temperate rural exposure; coastal salt air, acidic soils (pH below 5.5), and manure-heavy barn air all shorten life. When in doubt, go one coating class heavier—the cost difference on a fence order is typically under 8%.
      </p>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Whatever coating you choose, verify it at receiving: a magnetic coating-thickness gauge costs under $50 and reads zinc on steel directly. Check three points on three rolls; zinc below spec is the single most common cost-cutting trick in agricultural mesh imports.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What gauge wire is best for a chicken run?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            For a permanent outdoor run, use hexagonal mesh with 25 mm openings and 0.9 mm (BWG 20) wire in Class 3 galvanized or PVC-coated finish. For temporary runs or brooders, 0.7 mm (BWG 22) is acceptable. Whatever the gauge, bury a 20–30 cm apron or run an electric offset wire at ground level—predators dig under mesh long before they break through it.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Is fixed-knot fence worth the extra cost over hinge joint?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            For cattle, horses, and deer in permanent perimeter installations: yes. Fixed knot eliminates the flex-and-abrasion failure mode of hinge joints, holds post spacing under impact, and typically lasts twice as long—so the lifecycle cost per year is lower despite the 15–25% upfront premium. Hinge joint remains fine for sheep, interior cross-fencing, and short-term use.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">How long does galvanized field fence last?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            With standard hot-dip galvanizing (40–60 g/m² zinc), expect 5–10 years above ground in temperate climates and less in coastal or manure-heavy environments. Heavy galvanizing (200+ g/m²) or Galfan wire extends this to 20–30 years. Wire touching soil should always be the heavy class—buried zinc corrodes far faster than zinc in air.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Can I use the same wire mesh for a vineyard trellis and perimeter fence?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Not ideally. Trellis wire must be high-tensile (1,100 MPa+) so it holds fruiting-cane tension without stretching, while field fence uses lower-tensile wire that is easier to knot and handle. You can use the same heavy coating class for both, but keep the wire products separate—trellis wire is a distinct SKU in both factories and quotes.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What mesh size keeps out rabbits but lets chickens roam?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            A 25 mm hexagonal mesh with 0.7–0.9 mm wire handles both: the opening is small enough that rabbits cannot pass and chickens will not get their heads stuck (a risk with 40–50 mm mesh). If digging rabbits are the problem, add the buried apron—mesh size alone does not stop animals that go under.
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

